Doorbell should be a poll, not a push (Peter, 2026-08-13).

PROVEN TODAY by killing an xfce4-terminal window holding 3 tabs: astro-capture
(1d), astro-science (1d, mid-flight muppet meteor scan), aifabric-pane-driver
(2d23h). All 3 claude processes stayed ALIVE and Sl+, ptys still open and
writable, transcripts intact. But they are INERT - unreachable by every route:

  1. strand-mailbox send  -> rang MAILBOX.md, but the LIVE session's waiter
     consumed it. Doorbell is keyed to the FILE, not the session, so a second
     session on a strand silently eats the first's mail. One ring, first
     reader wins - it is a race.
  2. ding <pts>           -> does not type into the terminal at all; resolves
     pts -> its MAILBOX.md and writes there. Same dead end one layer down.
  3. raw write /dev/pts/N -> bytes land (exit 0) but NO turn: no tool children,
     no transcript write, CPU only the idle render loop. Tried CR, LF, LF+CR.
     Asked it to message back as a positive test: 150s, no reply.

ROOT CAUSE is not 'the terminal died'. Everything needed to work survived -
process, context, transcript, pty. Only the path that converts input into a
TURN died, because that path runs through the TUI. The expensive part (3 days
of context) survives; the cheap part (wiring) breaks; the expensive part is
lost anyway.

FIX: sessions POLL a file every ~5s instead of being pushed at. Inverts the
dependency - session reaches OUT to the filesystem, which does not care whether
a window exists. Cost is negligible: measured idle TUI render loop is already
13 ticks/6s just drawing a screen nobody watches; a stat() is noise beside it.
Also kills the collision (per-session read-offset has no race, both sessions
see the message) and makes 'please dcp' reachable in the one situation that
needs it - a session about to die, which is exactly when push-wake is gone.

CAVEAT: not a total fix. Needs the session to be BETWEEN turns to notice; one
wedged mid-tool-call still will not answer. Converts unconditional loss into
recoverable loss.

